## Where I would look first (IINA, general knowledge — all unverified)

- The play-slider's `IBAction` — usually named something like
  `playSliderChanged(_:)`, `playSliderAction(_:)`, or `seekSliderChanged(_:)`,
  on `MainWindowController` / `PlayerWindowController` or a custom
  `NSSlider`/touch-bar subclass.
- `PlayerCore.seek(...)` family — the methods that translate a target time
  into an mpv `seek` / `set time-pos` command.
- The end-of-file / playlist-advance handler — the code that responds to
  mpv's `end-file` event (or IINA's wrapper) and calls something like
  `playFile(at:)` / `navigateInPlaylist(...)`. Check whether seek-driven
  overshoot can reach that path.
- Any "scrubbing" / "seeking-by-user" boolean state on `PlayerCore` or
  `PlaybackInfo`. If one exists, the EOF/advance path should consult it.

## What I would do next (given Read/Grep)

1. Grep for `playSlider` to find the slider outlet and its action method.
2. Read the action method and confirm whether the time it computes is clamped
   against `info.videoDuration` before being passed to `PlayerCore.seek`.
3. Read `PlayerCore.seek(...)` and trace whether any branch interprets
   "target >= duration" as a trigger to advance the playlist (rather than
   simply forwarding to mpv and letting mpv clamp).
4. Find the handler for mpv's `end-file` event (likely in `MPVController` or
   `PlayerCore`) and confirm whether it can be entered as a side effect of a
   user-issued seek-past-end versus only true end-of-playback.
5. If a `PlaybackInfo.isSeeking` / `isScrubbing` flag exists, check whether
   the advance path consults it; if not, that's a strong candidate for the
   minimal fix site.

